\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Step-independent training fails through two mechanisms: information loss imposes a constant performance ceiling while sign misattribution causes cumulative degradation. A partial factorial experiment isolates each mechanism's contribution, and direct sign measurement confirms the theoretical prediction that credit agreement drops to near-random levels. Episode-level GRPO validates the diagnosis by addressing both failure modes, though multi-seed evaluation reveals that avoiding collapse is necessary but not sufficient---only 36\% of seeds achieve significant OOD generalization, pointing to initialization sensitivity as an important open problem. The dual-mechanism framework also explains why concurrent methods with finer-grained credit~\citep{hcapo2026,feng2025gigpo,turnppo2025} succeed by eliminating sign misattribution. Cross-scale (8B) and cross-environment (ScienceWorld) verification confirms these findings generalize beyond our primary setting.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Problem Formulation and GRPO}
\label{sec:problem_formulation}

We model the multi-turn agent as a partially observable Markov decision process (POMDP)~\citep{kaelbling1998planning} where the policy $\pi_\phi(a_t \mid h_t)$ generates actions conditioned on interaction history $h_t = (o_1, a_1, \ldots, o_t)$. Episodes of $T$ steps terminate with binary reward $R \in \{0, r_+\}$ (our experiments use a penalty variant with $r_- = -0.1$ per invalid action; Section~\ref{sec:setup}). GRPO~\citep{shao2024deepseekmath} samples $K$ rollouts per task and computes group-relative advantages:
\begin{align}
\hat{A}_k &= \frac{R_k - \mu}{\sigma + \epsilon}, \notag \\
\mu &= \frac{1}{K}\sum_{j=1}^K R_j, \quad \sigma = \sqrt{\frac{1}{K}\sum_{j=1}^K (R_j - \mu)^2}
\label{eq:grpo_advantage}
\end{align}
GRPO is episode-level by design: each rollout receives one return and one advantage, with no inherent temporal discounting.

\subsection{Step-Independent Windowing}
\label{sec:step_independent}

Agent RL frameworks must reconcile GRPO's episode-level design with the memory constraints of long trajectories. RAGEN~\citep{ragen2025} introduces \textbf{step-independent training}: the trajectory is partitioned into non-overlapping $h$-step windows, each receiving a discounted return:
\begin{equation}
R_k = \gamma^{(N - k)} \cdot R_{\text{episode}}
\label{eq:step_return}
\end{equation}
where $N = \lceil T/h \rceil$ is the number of windows. Each window is optimized as an independent GRPO sample. This reduces sequence length from $O(T)$ to $O(h)$ and multiplies the effective batch size, but structurally alters credit assignment.

\subsection{Dual Mechanism Analysis}
\label{sec:dual_mechanism}

Two mechanisms jointly cause training failure under step-independent optimization.

\subsubsection{Mechanism 1: Policy Information Loss}
\label{sec:m1_information_loss}

Step-independent training truncates context at both inference and training time. The policy $\pi_\phi(a_t \mid h_{\text{window}_k})$ conditions on only the local $h$-step history, losing access to earlier observations. With $h{=}2$, a window at step~8 has no access to the outcome of an exploration action at step~4, so the policy cannot learn strategies spanning more than $h$ steps. This imposes a \textit{constant} performance ceiling: no amount of optimization can recover absent information.

\subsubsection{Mechanism 2: Advantage Sign Misattribution}
\label{sec:m2_sign_misattribution}

Group normalization under step-independent training produces advantages whose sign reflects the \textit{episode} outcome, not local action quality:
\begin{equation}
\text{sign}(\hat{A}_k) \approx \text{sign}(R_{\text{episode}} - \mu)
\label{eq:sign_correlation}
\end{equation}
In failed episodes ($R_{\text{episode}} = 0$), all windows receive negative advantages when $\mu > 0$, including windows with locally correct actions. Conversely, successful episodes reinforce all windows, including those with suboptimal actions. Unlike M1's constant ceiling, sign misattribution is \textit{cumulative}: each wrong-sign gradient step further erodes policy competence. The discount factor $\gamma^{(N-k)}$ introduces magnitude decay across positions, but sign errors are strictly more harmful: magnitude decay slows convergence, while sign errors cause active divergence.

\subsection{Episode-Level Training}
\label{sec:episode_level}

Episode-level training addresses both mechanisms by treating the complete trajectory as a single GRPO sample. The full history $h_t$ is available at every step (eliminating M1), and a single advantage $\hat{A}$ applies uniformly to all tokens (eliminating M2). Uniform reinforcement of successful trajectories preserves good sub-strategies; window-level sign errors actively destroy them. This is GRPO's original design; step-independent windowing is the deviation.

\subsection{Partial Factorial Design}
\label{sec:factorial_design}

To separate the two mechanisms, we design a $2 \times 2$ partial factorial varying observation scope and advantage granularity: \textbf{Cell~A} (truncated $h{=}2$ + step-level advantage; both mechanisms active), \textbf{Cell~B} (truncated $h{=}2$ + episode-level advantage; only M1 active), \textbf{Cell~D} (full trajectory + episode-level advantage; neither active), and \textbf{Cell~C} (full trajectory + step-level advantage; only M2 active). Cell~C encountered persistent numerical instability across six hyperparameter configurations (Appendix~\ref{app:cell_c}); we therefore evaluate a partial 3-of-4 factorial. Comparing A vs.\ B isolates sign misattribution; comparing B vs.\ D isolates information loss.

